  "notes": "Sheet 0 (Fernando Cibrian, Vista Clara House, Cholula, Puebla; dusk exterior first): the house stands out from its neighbours by bold volumes - the main one, which gives access, is a 'clamp' of black-veined marble: two tall slabs framing floor-to-ceiling glass under a mirror-bright head; to its left a taller volume wrapped in gold-lit vertical timber slats, to its right pale stone and onyx piers, flat roofs, a reflecting pool starting at a large mirror in the vestibule and flowing in through the house, a dark timber and travertine interior with a double-height living room and perforated metal screens (sheets, interiors not modelled). Modelled: the stone body of three storeys, the slat volume, the black marble portal with its glass, marble and ebony piers, the pools, flanking buildings, planting. Guessed: every dimension, the depth of the house, the number of storeys at the back. A private home placed at its town only (Cholula)."
